Converts a construct template (with slots and control markers intact) to an op-list that the C++ firmware header consumes to reproduce plain movement, smart closer-dropping, and nvim tabstops. Surface text (`Lit`, `Brace`, `Tab`) coalesces into single `Text` ops; slots, landings, and hard newlines each break the run. The C++ fills the k-th `Slot` op with the k-th template-order slot text from `walk_construct`.

`template_ops(template: &[Chunk]) -> Vec<TemplateOp>` is the sole public entry point; it walks the input chunks and builds the op-list in template order. Adjacent text coalesces via `push_text`, which appends to the last `Text` op if one exists, otherwise creates a new one. `Chunk::Landing`, `Chunk::Newline`, and `Chunk::BodyBreak` map to `TemplateOp::Landing`, `TemplateOp::Newline` respectively; any chunk matched by `is_slot` becomes `TemplateOp::Slot`. The function never panics and silently drops unresolved chunks that should not survive Pass A.